name: xargs and doing it to everything
boss: Swarm
flavor: A thousand commands. One directive.
brief: Some commands read filenames as arguments; pipes carry text on stdin.
brief: xargs is the adaptor between them: it turns lines of input into
brief: arguments of a command.
brief:
brief: The classic trap is filenames with spaces. find -print0 emits names
brief: separated by a NUL byte, which cannot occur in a filename, and xargs -0
brief: reads them back. Use that pair by default and the problem never arises.
---
q: find every .log file and count the lines of each, using xargs
a: find . -name '*.log' | xargs wc -l
out: [0-9]
ref: xargs
teach: xargs
---
q: do the same, safely, for filenames that might contain spaces
a: find . -name '*.log' -print0 | xargs -0 wc -l
out: [0-9]
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -0
hint: NUL is the one byte a filename cannot contain
---
q: pass exactly one argument per command invocation
a: ls | xargs -n 1 echo
out: .
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -n
---
q: put each input where you want it, not just at the end, using a placeholder
a: ls *.log | xargs -I {} echo found {}
out: ^found .*\.log$
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -I
---
q: show each command before running it
a: ls | xargs -t echo
out: .
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -t
---
q: do nothing at all when the input is empty, rather than running once
a: printf '' | xargs -r echo nothing
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -r
hint: without -r, an empty input still runs the command once with no arguments
---
q: stop reading input at the word STOP
a: printf 'a b STOP c' | xargs -E STOP echo
out: ^a b$
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -E
---
q: run at most 2 commands at the same time
a: ls | xargs -P 2 -n 1 echo
out: .
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -P
---
q: ask before running each command
a: ls | xargs -p echo
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -p
---
q: take the argument list from %txt% rather than standard input
a: xargs -a %txt% echo
out: rotate
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -a
---
q: keep each command line under 100 bytes, splitting into more invocations
a: ls | xargs -s 100 echo
out: .
ref: xargs
teach: xargs -s
---
q: strip the directory part off the path %dir%/%txt%
a: basename %dir%/%txt%
out: ^%txt%$
ref: basename
teach: basename
---
q: strip the .txt suffix as well as the directory
a: basename %txt% .txt
out: .
ref: basename
teach: basename
---
q: strip a suffix using the explicit flag rather than a second argument
a: basename -s .txt %txt%
out: .
ref: basename
teach: basename -s
---
q: take the basename of several paths at once
a: basename -a %txt% %log%
out: .
ref: basename
teach: basename -a
---
q: print the directory part of %dir%/%txt%
a: dirname %dir%/%txt%
out: ^%dir%$
ref: dirname
teach: dirname
---
q: resolve %txt% to its full absolute path
a: realpath %txt%
out: ^/
ref: realpath
teach: realpath
---
q: follow a symlink to whatever it finally points at
a: readlink -f %txt%
out: ^/
ref: readlink
teach: readlink -f
---
q: read a symlink without a trailing newline
a: readlink -n %txt%
ref: readlink
teach: readlink -n
---
q: where does the command sed live
a: which sed
out: sed
ref: which
teach: which
---
q: is sed a builtin, a file, or a function
a: type sed
out: sed
ref: type
teach: type
---
q: remember where a command lives so the shell stops searching for it
a: hash sed
ref: hash
teach: hash
---
q: check whether sed is available, the portable way
a: command -v sed
out: sed
ref: command
teach: command -v
